import "core/list.ct"
import "core/webdoc.ct"


module(Catln/Doc)
  # # A display a library renders for itself
    A [Show/Rendered] is a value together with the files that draw it. It is the third way a library
    says what one of its types looks like, and the first that can say what one *does*.
    The two before it each give up something. Membership of [DShow] hands the value itself to the
    webdocs, which then need a component named after the type — so the display lives in the doc
    server rather than in the library, which is what [Show/Html] is written against. An arm of
    [/Catln/Doc/dshow] building a [Show/Html] keeps the display in the library, but what it builds is
    markup and nothing more: a tree that folds when it is clicked needs a script, and markup laid
    into a page brings none. A browser does not run a script written into markup handed to it, and an
    arm joining strings together has nowhere to put one in any case.
    So this carries both halves. The [value] is handed over whole, as membership would, and the
    [assets] name the JavaScript and the CSS that turn it into a display. Both live in the library's
    own directory beside the pages declaring the type, and the doc server fetches them without
    knowing what they are for.
  data(Show/Rendered[$V](value -> $V, assets -> /Data/List[$T -> String]))
    # [value] is the value being shown, of whatever type the library is showing — a type parameter
      and not [DShow], because a type with an arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshow] is by that fact not a member
      of [DShow], and a field asking for one could not hold it.
      [assets] are paths under the standard library, as an import writes them: `json/json.js` sits
      beside `json/json.ct`. A `.js` is loaded as a module and asked for its `render(root, value)`;
      a `.css` is laid on the page once. The doc server serves them and reads neither.
      Nothing here says which asset renders which value. A library naming two scripts is a library
      whose scripts sort that out between them, which is the same freedom it has over its own pages.

  every(Show/Rendered.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  # # A call shown as what it did, step by step
    A [Show/Steps] is a call displayed as a row per step of it: what the step was handed, and what the
    call made of that. It is the first display in this file that is of a call rather than of a value,
    which is what a library reaches for when the interesting thing about one of its functions is not
    what the function returns but how it got there.
    The three displays above it each answer "what does this value look like". A mapping over a list
    answers that with the list it produced, which is the one thing about it a reader could already see
    without asking. What they cannot see is which element became which, and that is a shape — an
    ordered correspondence — rather than a value, so it takes a display of its own.
    A library writes one of these by writing an arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall] whose argument is a call
    of the function, as it writes an arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshow] whose argument is a value of a type.
    Nothing else is needed and nothing marks the function: the arm is the whole declaration, and a
    function with no such arm has no display of its own and is shown the way everything else is. See
    [/Data/List] in @core/list-webdocs.ct@, which is where the list library's are written.
    A show of a call PERFORMS the call. It is handed the call's own arguments and nothing else — not a
    result computed elsewhere and passed in — so a step's output is what the arm works out for that
    step. That is what makes it a display of the function rather than of something the function left
    behind, and it is why an arm needs no cooperation from whatever is showing it.
  data(Show/Step(index -> Integer, input -> String, output -> String))
    # One row: which step it is, what that step was handed, and what came back. [index] counts from
      zero, as a reader of a list counts.
      The two ends are [String] and not the values themselves. Every display in this file carries what
      it shows already rendered, and a row here holds a number beside two values of two types the row
      knows nothing about — so a field holding both of them would be a list over [DShow], and
      expanding that open class over a row's worth of unrelated types is the interaction
      @core/data.ct@ already records going badly for [/Data/Option]. The arm building the row renders
      each end through [/Data/toString], which is the same rendering a [#print] of either would get.
      The cost is stated where it is paid: an arm can only show steps whose ends are [/Data/ToString],
      and is written to match only those.
  data(Show/Steps(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> Show/Step..]))
    # The rows, in the order the call took them.
      No columns and no headings: what the two ends of a step mean is the same question in every call
      that has steps at all — this is what went in, this is what came out — so naming them per call
      would be every arm writing the same two words. What draws the rows says it once.
      Nor is there an arrow field, for the same reason. A row points from one end to the other by
      being a row, and the glyph that says so belongs to whatever draws it.

  every(Show/Steps.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

  data(Show/FoldStep(index -> Integer, acc -> String, input -> String, output -> String))
    # One row of a fold: which step it is, the accumulator it was handed, the element it combined
      into it, and the accumulator it handed on. The ends are rendered text for the reason
      [Show/Step]'s are.
  data(Show/FoldSteps(init -> String, steps -> /Data/List[$T -> Show/FoldStep], result -> String, fromRight -> Boolean))
    # A fold shown as what it did: its rows, in element order, between the accumulator it started
      from and the one it ended with.
      A second display rather than [Show/Steps] with a column more. A step of a fold has three ends
      rather than two, and what makes it a fold is where each step's output goes: into the next
      step's accumulator, and from the last step into the result. That is a line between rows, not
      anything in one, so it takes a display that draws one.
      [fromRight] says which way that line runs, since a fold from the right starts at the last
      element and hands each output to the step before it. It is not a glyph: which arrow says so
      belongs to whatever draws the rows, as it does for [Show/Steps]. The rows stay in element order
      either way, which is also the order a debugger finds the calls that made them in.

  every(Show/FoldSteps.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

# # Why this is not written in core/webdoc.ct
  [assets] is a [/Data/List], and @core/webdoc.ct@ may not name one. That page is reached from
  @core/compile.ct@, which every program reaches, so an import of @core/list.ct@ written there closes
  the cycle @core/result.ct@ measures at seven hundred seconds. This page sits where @core/result.ct@
  sits for the same reason and by the same arrangement: it reaches @core/list.ct@ freely, and only
  @core/main.ct@ reaches it.
# # What a doc server does not do with these files
  A library's assets are not pages. Nothing reads them, nothing watches them, and the extensions a
  read knows ([Syntax.InferImport.isSupportedFileExtension]) do not include them. So a script edited
  while a doc server is running changes nothing until the reader loads the page again — the server
  has not noticed, and the browser is holding the module it already fetched.
